---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Code from '../components/Code.astro';
import { release, launch, launchLabel, sourceInstall, sourceUrl } from '../lib/site';
const sections = ['Homebrew', 'From source', 'Requirements', 'Update', 'Uninstall', 'Troubleshooting'];
---
<Base title="Install" description="Verified Homebrew and source installation for NMSh, with shell requirements, platform maturity, updating and safe removal.">
  <div class="container"><header class="page-hero"><span class="eyebrow">Get started / {launchLabel}</span><h1>Keep your shell.<br />Add NMSh.</h1><p class="lead">Start it in the terminal you already use. You do not need an account or a different login shell.</p></header>
    <div class="article-layout"><nav class="article-nav" aria-label="Installation sections">{sections.map(section => <a href={`#${section.toLowerCase().replaceAll(' ', '-')}`}>{section}</a>)}</nav><article class="prose">
      <h2 id="homebrew">Homebrew <a class="heading-anchor" href="#homebrew" aria-label="Link to Homebrew">#</a></h2><p>Recommended on macOS. Homebrew manages the installation and its dependencies.</p><Code code={'brew install raiseCatError/tap/nmsh\nnmsh'} label="macOS / Install" />
      {release.tapVersion !== `v${launch.version}` && <aside class="note"><strong>Homebrew does not yet provide this launch baseline.</strong><p>Verified {release.verifiedAt}: the formula references {release.tapVersion}. The latest published stable release is {release.latest.tag}. The source instructions below target the {launchLabel}, not the tap. <a href="https://github.com/raiseCatError/homebrew-tap/blob/HEAD/Formula/nmsh.rb">Inspect the formula ↗</a></p></aside>}
      <h2 id="from-source">Build from source <a class="heading-anchor" href="#from-source" aria-label="Link to source build">#</a></h2><p>{launch.state === 'candidate' ? 'This checks out the exact public candidate commit used by these docs. It is not a published release. The final tag will replace this checkpoint after release validation.' : 'This checks out the verified published release tag.'} Run these commands in an ordinary shell, outside NMSh.</p><Code code={sourceInstall} label={`Source / ${launchLabel}`} /><p>Allow the required <code>node-pty</code> native install step. Depending on the platform, a C/C++ build toolchain and Python may be needed. The source distribution is not an npm registry installer.</p>
      <h2 id="requirements">Requirements <a class="heading-anchor" href="#requirements" aria-label="Link to requirements">#</a></h2><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Platform and runtime requirements" tabindex="0"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Component</th><th scope="col">Requirement / evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Node.js</th><td>{release.requirements.node} for NMSh</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Shell</th><td>zsh (default), Bash 4.4+ or Fish. macOS Bash 3.2 is too old.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">macOS</th><td>Primary platform. Physical QA for this latest release remains pending.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Linux</th><td>Beta. Ubuntu automated suite and Fedora subset; physical QA pending.</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Windows</th><td>WSL 2 beta path only. Run Linux Node inside WSL. Native Windows and WSL 1 unsupported.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Keep a real shell as your system/login shell. <strong>Do not set NMSh using <code>chsh</code>.</strong> An optional terminal launch command starts the frontend without changing your shell identity.</p><p><a href="/docs/shells/">Detailed shell and host compatibility →</a></p>
      <h2 id="update">Update <a class="heading-anchor" href="#update" aria-label="Link to updating">#</a></h2><Code code="brew upgrade raiseCatError/tap/nmsh" label="Homebrew / Update" /><p>For a Homebrew install, <code>/update</code> directs you back to Homebrew rather than modifying its Cellar. For a clean official source checkout, <code>/update</code> shows the latest stable plan; <code>/update apply</code> applies it with build verification and rollback on failure. Review the plan first.</p>{launch.state === 'candidate' && <p>The detached candidate checkout above is for review. Do not use the stable updater to advance between candidate commits. Use a fresh checkout of the next explicitly reviewed commit instead.</p>}
      <h2 id="uninstall">Uninstall <a class="heading-anchor" href="#uninstall" aria-label="Link to uninstalling">#</a></h2><p>Finish live work first; closing a window can leave its session running. Homebrew installations are removed through Homebrew:</p><Code code="brew uninstall nmsh" label="Homebrew / Remove" /><p>For source installations, preview NMSh's ownership-aware removal plan:</p><Code code={'nmsh uninstall --dry-run\nnmsh uninstall'} label="Source / Preview, then confirm" /><p>The source uninstaller removes only launcher links it proves belong to NMSh and refuses while live sessions run. Do not casually delete the config directory: saved sessions and preferences live there. Review any separate data-removal choice explicitly.</p>
      <h2 id="troubleshooting">Troubleshooting <a class="heading-anchor" href="#troubleshooting" aria-label="Link to install troubleshooting">#</a></h2><Code code={'node --version\nnmsh --version\nnmsh doctor'} label="Diagnostics" /><p>GUI terminals can inherit a different PATH. For Ghostty, the canonical guide gives this pattern, using paths you replace with your actual installed locations:</p><Code code="command = direct:/absolute/path/to/node /absolute/path/to/nmsh" lang="text" label="Ghostty config / Example paths only" /><p>Do not paste placeholder paths unchanged. Confirm the Node executable, launcher location and host configuration before changing a launch command.</p><p>See <a href="/docs/troubleshooting/">troubleshooting</a>, the <a href={sourceUrl('README.md')}>canonical install instructions</a> and <a href="/releases/">release limitations</a>. There are no official downloadable native installers advertised here.</p>
    </article></div>
  </div>
</Base>
